<%#
  One declarable fact, rendered by name.

  A partial rather than four blocks in the form, so that a field's label and
  hint are written once. It is rendered from one place now that the disclosure
  is gone, and it stays a partial because the form is assembled from a list the
  controller computes rather than from a fixed set of fields.

  Labels and hints come through `tax_fact_label` / `tax_fact_hint` rather than
  from `t` directly, so that a rule with something of its own to say about a
  fact says it. The opening date is the case that forced this: the same
  sentence cannot describe a PEA's five-year clock and a PER's vintage.

  Keys are given in full rather than through the lazy `t(".x")` form. Lazy
  lookup resolves against the template that happens to be rendering, and these
  strings belong to the fact, not to this partial.
%>
<div class="space-y-1">
  <% if fact == :product %>
    <%= f.select :product,
                 [ [ t("tax.profiles.fields.product.from_sure"), "" ] ] + product_choices,
                 { label: tax_fact_label(fact, rule_id) } %>
  <% elsif fact == :opened_on %>
    <%= f.date_field :opened_on, label: tax_fact_label(fact, rule_id) %>
  <% else %>
    <%# The currency is in the label rather than assumed. These figures are
        copied off a statement in the account's own currency, and the report
        converts them; leaving the label bare invites entering euros into a
        dollar account and getting a confidently wrong answer back. %>
    <%= f.number_field fact, step: "0.01",
                       label: tax_fact_label(fact, rule_id, currency: account.currency) %>
  <% end %>

  <p class="text-xs text-secondary">
    <%= tax_fact_hint(fact, rule_id, currency: account.currency) %>
  </p>

  <%# What Sure knows about the date, which is one of two quite different
      things and is never pre-filled into the box.

      Pre-filling would make Sure's answer look like something the reader had
      declared, and the two are treated differently: a declared date overrides,
      an anchor is deferred to. An empty field keeps that difference visible
      and keeps clearing the override possible.

      The field is offered either way. An earlier version dropped it from the
      form whenever Sure had a date, which was defensible while the disclosure
      below still held it and became a trap once that went: the household's own
      date is the one the law cares about -- the first payment in, not the day
      a balance was first recorded -- and there has to be somewhere to say so. %>
  <% if fact == :opened_on && sure_opening_date.present? %>
    <p class="text-xs text-secondary">
      <%= t("tax.profiles.fields.opened_on.from_sure",
            date: l(sure_opening_date, format: :long)) %>
    </p>
  <% elsif fact == :opened_on && sure_known_since.present? %>
    <%# A floor, and said as such. An opening anchor carrying a balance proves
        the account existed before that date without saying when it began, so
        it is offered as the bound it is -- context for someone reaching for a
        statement, never a value to accept. See Tax::SubjectBuilder#known_since. %>
    <p class="text-xs text-secondary">
      <%= t("tax.profiles.fields.opened_on.known_since",
            date: l(sure_known_since, format: :long)) %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
</div>
